## Part 1: How EDRChoker Actually Works (And Why Detection Matters)


EDRChoker operates at the NDIS layer using `New-NetQosPolicy` to create Quality of Service throttle policies. Instead of blocking traffic (which generates firewall logs), it starves bandwidth:

```powershell
New-NetQosPolicy -Name "policy-name" `
  -AppPathNameMatchCondition "C:\Program Files\...\Defender.exe" `
  -ThrottleRateActionBitsPerSecond 8  # 8 bits/second = death by a thousand timeouts


https://github.com/SlimKQL/Detections.AI/blob/main/KQL/edrchoker-detection.kql#L1

EDRChoker is a publicly disclosed red-team tool that uses Windows' Policy-based QoS (pacer.sys) to throttle EDR agent processes down to as low as 8 bits per second, causing their connections to the server to consistently time out and effectively isolating the agent. It operates at the NDIS layer, below where Windows Firewall/WFP-based blocking works, and the created QoS policies persist across reboots.


